<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Annual Customs of Dahomey</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Xwetanu, Huetanu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Annual Customs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> were the main yearly celebration of the Kingdom of Dahomey, held at its capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abomey">Abomey</a>, and largely begun under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agaja">King Agaja</a> around 1730. Every family lineage was expected to bring gifts and tribute to the king, who displayed the wealth and then redistributed much of it. The ceremony honoured the royal ancestors with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a>: captives from war and criminals were killed, around 500 at a time, mostly by decapitation, and as many as 4,000 were reported killed in 1727. The Customs also held military parades and the Great Council, where leaders, women among them, debated policy until the king declared consensus.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/annual-customs-of-dahomey.jpg" alt="Annual Customs of Dahomey" width="330" height="284" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Archibad Dalzel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Victims_for_sacrifice-1793.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Among the Fon and other peoples of the region, family lineages traditionally gathered for feasts at which members gave gifts to their eldest, discussed family matters and, as part of Vodun practice, made gifts and sacrifices to the spirits and ancestors. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/houegbadja">Houegbadja</a>, who reigned around 1645 to 1685, made such offerings to the spirits a matter for the king alone.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agaja">King Agaja</a>, who reigned from 1718 to 1740, centred the ceremonies on the king and the royal dynasty. The Customs became the central religious ceremony of the kingdom, wide participation was required, every lineage owed gifts and tribute, sometimes regarded as taxes, and animal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a> was added. Family ceremonies were not allowed until the royal one was over. Later kings made the Customs larger, longer and more lavish, and some features may date from the 19th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The main religious purpose was to give thanks to the ancestors of the royal lineage and win their approval. The Fon name xwetanu means literally yearly head business, after the decapitation of the victims, and in later years the rite also included the spilling of the sacrificed people's blood. A new king had to hold a large ceremony in honour of his predecessor to complete the funeral rites; until then he was held not to have the ancestors' approval.</p><p>The scholar John C. Yoder sets out the sequence of days: a procession from Cana, private receptions and petitions, parades through the Great Square, historical songs by court singers, the parade and distribution of the king's wealth, reviews of the troops, the meetings of the Great Council, oaths of loyalty by the troops, and a mock battle and slave raid staged by the Mino. Officials spent their evenings building coalitions of support, and the Customs closed with gifts to officials and answers to petitions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Yoder has argued that the Customs served a political function similar to that of parliaments in Western countries, though far from the open contests of democracies: in the Great Council low-ranking officials could publicly rebuke high-ranking ones. Objects associated with the Customs are kept at the Abomey Historical Museum in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-palaces-of-abomey">Royal Palaces of Abomey</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Annual Customs of Dahomey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
